UX Improvements
Critical UX Bugs

1. "Already a member" is invisible feedback (BUG)
   When a logged-in user who is already a member clicks "Join Team" on /join/T253QL, the 23505 duplicate key error is silently caught and the user is redirected as if they just joined for the first time. There is no "You're already on this team!" message. A confused parent clicking their old invite link will be silently redirected with no explanation.
2. Post-login redirect for invite/join codes is broken (BUG)
   When a new (unauthenticated) user clicks "Create Account & Join" or "Sign In & Join" on either /join/$code or /invite/$token, the code is saved to sessionStorage. However, there is no evidence in the root route, the auth routes, or an auth listener that picks up join_code or invite_token from sessionStorage after login/signup completes and automatically re-runs the join. If the user logs in and the redirect logic doesn't trigger automatically, they land on /teams and the join never happens. This is the single most critical user-facing bug.
3. Join Code page doesn't detect "already a member" before showing the button
   A user who is already on the team sees the standard "Join The Team / JOIN TEAM" UI. There is no check upfront to show "You're already a member of this team → Go to team". This is confusing and potentially causes silent duplicate-insert suppression to be mistaken for a successful join.
4. Invite page title is wrong for Role Invites
   Both /join/$code and /invite/$token share the Helmet title "Join Team | Dugout". The invite page says "YOU'RE INVITED" but the browser tab still says "Join Team." This is inconsistent and confusing to users with multiple tabs open.
5. No success state after joining
   Neither the join code nor the invite flow shows a "Welcome to the team! 🎉" screen after joining. The user is immediately redirected to the schedule page. First-time users don't know what they're looking at.
6. Expected members have no edit capability
   The "Add Expected Member" form creates a placeholder with name + optional note. There is no way to edit a name or note once created — only delete. A coach who misspells a player's name must delete and re-add.
7. Expected members have no matching/transition mechanism
   The expected member system has no concept of "claiming" a placeholder. When a new user joins, they are added as a separate real roster entry while the expected member placeholder remains in "PENDING" forever. There is no automatic or manual linking between the two. This defeats the purpose of expected members.
8. Role Invite has no confirmation after generating
   After generating a role invite link, the user sees a copy link button and a "Create Another" link, but no option to revoke the invite. There is no invite management view — once you generate it, you can't see all active invites or cancel them.
9. The "Invite to Team" modal title is generic
   The bottom sheet says "INVITE TO TEAM" but offers two fundamentally different invite methods (shareable code vs. role-specific token). Users have no guidance on which to use when and why.
10. "Reset Code" on Join Code tab has no confirmation dialog
    Clicking "Reset Code" immediately generates a new code, invalidating the old one. Any existing links using the previous code silently stop working. There's no warning about this.
11. Missing player count / context on invite page
    When a parent/player opens a join link, they only see the team name, sport, and season. There is no coach name, player count, or other context that would build confidence the link is legitimate.

