Security Findings
Critical
SEC-1: Anyone with a join code becomes a parent
The joinTeamByCode service hardcodes role: "parent" regardless of who is joining. A coach, player, or manager using the join code will all become parents. There is no role differentiation at the join-code level. The code says "Anyone can use this code to join" which is true — and problematic.
SEC-2: Invite token TOCTOU (Race Condition)
acceptInvite does:

getInviteByToken(token) — checks used_at IS NULL and expires_at
insert into team_members
update team_invites SET used_at = now()

Between steps 1 and 3 there is a window where the same token can be accepted by two users simultaneously. Both will pass step 1, both will insert, and both will succeed (the second insert will be caught by the unique constraint and silently return the teamId — but both users are now members). The fix is an atomic DB transaction or using a Supabase RPC with FOR UPDATE lock.
SEC-3: Expiry only checked client-side
getInviteByToken filters .is("used_at", null) in the DB query (good), but the expires_at check is:
jsif (data.expires_at && new Date(data.expires_at) < new Date()) return null;
This runs in the browser. A skilled attacker can disable this JS check and accept expired invites. The DB query does not include .lt("expires_at", new Date().toISOString()). This should be a DB-level filter.
SEC-4: getSession() trusts localStorage tokens
useAuth() calls supabase.auth.getSession() which reads from localStorage without re-verifying with Supabase. supabase.auth.getUser() should be used for any security-sensitive check as it re-validates with the server. This is already done in the service layer (joinTeamByCode, acceptInvite) but not in the UI guard layer.
SEC-5: Join code enumeration is trivially possible
The code space is 32 characters × 6 positions = ~1 billion combinations, but the character set (A-Z without O/I, 2-9) gives only 32^6 = ~1 billion. Since there's no rate-limiting on getTeamByJoinCode, an attacker could enumerate valid codes. The Supabase RLS should enforce that team_join_codes is not publicly readable (only the team being joined should be returned).
SEC-6: No authorization check on invite creation
createInvite relies on Supabase RLS to reject unauthorized inserts (per the comment "enforced by RLS"). If RLS is misconfigured, any authenticated user could generate invites for any team. There's no client-side guard (e.g., checking the user is a coach/admin before showing the invite button) as defense-in-depth.

Performance Findings
PERF-1: Duplicate getUser() calls in join/accept flows
Both joinTeamByCode and acceptInvite call supabase.auth.getUser() and also call getTeamByJoinCode/getInviteByToken internally. This results in 2–3 sequential network round-trips before the insert.
PERF-2: useAuth() has staleTime: Infinity
The auth query never refetches. If a token expires mid-session, the user appears logged in to the UI but all API calls will fail. There's no onAuthStateChange listener in the query key invalidation path.
PERF-3: No optimistic updates on join
When the user clicks "Join Team," there's a loading state but no optimistic roster update. The page refetches from the server after success.
PERF-4: useJoinCode staleTime is 5 minutes
If a coach resets the code, another user who loaded the old code page within 5 minutes will try to join with a stale (now invalid) code, causing a confusing error.

Code Quality Findings
CQ-1: Inconsistent error handling patterns
Service functions mix throw new Error(error.message) with return null. Callers must handle both null returns and thrown exceptions, making error propagation unpredictable.
CQ-2: Array.isArray(invite.teams) defensive check is unnecessary
Supabase join relations return a single object for !inner joins, not an array. This defensive code suggests confusion about the schema shape and may mask future bugs.
CQ-3: joinTeamByCode hardcodes role
tsrole: "parent" // BUG: should be dynamic or configurable
The join-code flow has no role context. This is a design decision masquerading as an implementation detail.
CQ-4: expectedMembers service has no updateExpectedMember
Only addExpectedMember and deleteExpectedMember exist. The UI cannot edit an expected member. This is a missing feature that directly maps to missing code.
CQ-5: Session storage key cleanup missing
After a user logs in via the join/invite flow, the join_code and invite_token items in sessionStorage are never cleared. They persist for the session, meaning re-visiting the login page could trigger a spurious join attempt.
CQ-6: No Zod validation at service layer
addExpectedMember accepts name: string but performs no validation before insert. An empty string "" could potentially be inserted if the UI validation is bypassed.
CQ-7: useAuth uses getSession not getUser
Documented above in security. Worth repeating as a code quality concern — should be standardized across all auth checks.

